---
Part XI, Chapter 5: Dev releases
Package: scripts/
Language: TypeScript
---

# Dev releases

A public release waits for a milestone, and a milestone can take months. A project that builds on Loom's packages cannot always wait that long. It needs the work of last week, and it needs that work under a version it can pin, so that its own build gives the same result each time.

A dev release supplies that version. It is a build of one commit, published under a number of its own to a registry that runs on the developer's machine and answers only that machine. No part of a dev release reaches npm or GitHub, so the last public release stays the public Loom until the next milestone.

Two tools make a dev release. The `registry` tool runs the local registry, and `bun run registry` starts it. The `release-local` tool publishes a commit into that registry, and `bun run release-local` runs it.

## Where the registry listens

The local registry answers at one address, port 4873 on `localhost`, the name a machine gives itself. A request from another machine never reaches a server that listens on `localhost`, so the address alone keeps the registry private. Both tools read the address from the `registry` constant. The `registry` tool listens there, and the `release-local` tool publishes there.

=>

const registry = 'http://localhost:4873'

## What the registry holds [registry.yaml]

The registry is Verdaccio, a small npm registry made to run on one machine. Verdaccio reads its settings from a configuration file, and this section is that file.

The `storage` setting names the folder where Verdaccio keeps each package it holds. The path is relative, and Verdaccio resolves it against the folder of the configuration file, so the packages land beside the configuration.

The `max_body_size` setting is the largest request Verdaccio accepts, and its default of ten megabytes is too small for Loom. A dev release publishes the standalone executable as a package of about thirty megabytes, and npm sends a package to a registry as text, which makes it larger by a third. So the limit here is a hundred megabytes.

The registry keeps one account. npm never publishes without signing in, and Verdaccio turns away a token it did not issue, so the `release-local` tool signs in before it publishes. Under `auth`, `max_users: 1` lets one account register and no second, and that one account is the tool's. Verdaccio keeps the account in the `htpasswd` file, beside the configuration.

The `npmjs` uplink is the public registry. Verdaccio asks the uplink for the packages and the versions it does not hold itself, and keeps a copy of what the uplink sends back.

The `packages` setting says who may do what, by package name. Any client may install a package in the `@athrio` scope, since its `access` is open to `$all`, Verdaccio's word for every client, signed in or not. Only a signed-in client may publish such a package or remove it again, and removing it is how a broken dev release is withdrawn. The scope is also proxied to the uplink. The registry therefore answers under one package name for the dev releases it holds and for the public releases npm holds.

Every other package is read through from npm. Its rule names nobody who may publish, and Verdaccio then lets nobody, so nothing outside the `@athrio` scope can be published here.

The executable's package has a rule of its own, at the top of the list, because Verdaccio applies the first rule whose pattern matches a name. The package is named `loom-` followed by a system and an architecture, such as `@athrio/loom-darwin-x64`. npm has never held such a package. A rule with an uplink would still have Verdaccio ask npm about it whenever its own copy was more than two minutes old, and every Loom command on a dev build asks the registry about this package. So its rule names no uplink, and Verdaccio answers from what it holds, without leaving the machine.

The `log` setting keeps Verdaccio's output to warnings and errors.

=>

storage: ./storage

max_body_size: 100mb

auth:
  htpasswd:
    file: ./htpasswd
    max_users: 1

uplinks:
  npmjs:
    url: https://registry.npmjs.org/

packages:
  '@athrio/loom-{darwin,linux,win32}-*':
    access: $all
    publish: $authenticated
    unpublish: $authenticated
  '@athrio/*':
    access: $all
    publish: $authenticated
    unpublish: $authenticated
    proxy: npmjs
  '**':
    access: $all
    proxy: npmjs

log: { type: stdout, format: pretty, level: warn }

## What the registry tool draws on

The `registry` tool copies a file and starts a process, so it draws on Effect's filesystem service and on Bun's process spawning. Node's `os` names the home folder, and `path` and `url` find the configuration file beside the tool's own module.

=>

import { Console, Effect, FileSystem } from 'effect'
import { BunRuntime, BunServices } from '@effect/platform-bun'
import { homedir } from 'node:os'
import { dirname, join } from 'node:path'
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url'

## Starting the registry

The registry keeps everything it owns in one folder, `~/.loom-dev/registry`: its configuration, and under `storage` every package it holds. The folder lies outside every repository, so a dev release outlives the checkout it was built from. Deleting the folder empties the registry.

`configured` makes that folder and copies this chapter's configuration into it, and it does so at every start. The registry therefore always runs under the configuration the book gives, and a change to this chapter reaches the registry the next time it starts.

`serving` runs Verdaccio in the foreground until someone stops it, the way a development server runs. Nothing installs Verdaccio beforehand. The `verdaccio` constant names one version. Bun's `bunx` command fetches that version the first time it is asked for, and keeps it for the starts that follow. Verdaccio runs in the registry's folder, under the copied configuration, and listens at the `registry` address. When Verdaccio exits, the tool exits with Verdaccio's exit code.

=>

const verdaccio = 'verdaccio@7.1.2'

const home = join(homedir(), '.loom-dev', 'registry')

const configured = Effect.gen(function* () {
  const fs = yield* FileSystem.FileSystem
  const config = join(home, 'config.yaml')
  yield* fs.makeDirectory(home, { recursive: true })
  yield* fs.copyFile(join(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), 'registry.yaml'), config)
  return config
})

const serving = (config: string): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  Effect.promise(
    () =>
      Bun.spawn(['bunx', verdaccio, '--config', config, '--listen', registry], {
        cwd: home,
        stdout: 'inherit',
        stderr: 'inherit',
      }).exited,
  ).pipe(
    Effect.flatMap((code) =>
      Effect.sync(() => {
        process.exitCode = code
      }),
    ),
  )

BunRuntime.runMain(
  configured.pipe(
    Effect.tap(() =>
      Console.log(`the registry answers at ${registry} and keeps its packages in ${home}`),
    ),
    Effect.flatMap(serving),
    Effect.provide(BunServices.layer),
  ),
)

