## What a dev release publishes

A dev release publishes eight packages. Three are the ones a public release publishes to npm: the command-line tool, the TypeScript service, and the tropes. Four more are the AST, the configuration, the contract of the language services, and the language. npm has never held those four, because a public release bundles them into the packages it does publish. A project that reads a loom in a program of its own imports them directly, so a dev release publishes them as packages.

The eighth package holds Loom's standalone executable, built for the machine that makes the release. A public release attaches an executable for each platform to its release on GitHub. A dev release is made on one machine and used on that machine, so it builds the one executable the machine runs and keeps it in the registry beside the other seven, where `loom upgrade` finds it. The constant `executablePackage` names the package for the platform the executable runs on: `loom-darwin-x64` on an Intel Mac, which the registry holds as `@athrio/loom-darwin-x64`.

The list `devPackages` holds the seven source packages in the order they depend on one another, so each package is published after every package it requires. The list names each package by its folder under `packages`, and the package's name on the registry is that folder's name in the `@athrio` scope. The list `releasedPackages` adds the executable's package at the end, since the executable is compiled from the command-line tool.

The `release-local` tool asks Git for a commit, asks the installed `loom` command to set a version and to upgrade itself, and asks Bun and npm to compile, test, pack and publish, so it spawns processes through Bun. It reads the workspace's configuration through `LoomConfig`, the service Part V describes. Effect's `Schema` reads the registry's answer, and `Option` carries a version that may not parse. Effect's filesystem service makes the folder the release is built in and writes the executable's package there. Node's `path` and `url` find the workspace around the tool's own module.

=>

import { Array, Console, Data, Effect, FileSystem, Option, Schema, pipe } from 'effect'
import { BunRuntime, BunServices } from '@effect/platform-bun'
import { LoomConfig } from '@athrio/loom-config/LoomConfig'
import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path'
import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url'

const workspace = resolve(dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url)), '..')

const devPackages: ReadonlyArray<string> = [
  'loom-ast',
  'loom-config',
  'loom-lang-services',
  'loom-lang',
  'loom-service-typescript',
  'loom-tropes',
  'loom',
]

const executablePackage = `loom-${process.platform}-${process.arch}`

const releasedPackages: ReadonlyArray<string> = [...devPackages, executablePackage]

## Running a command

`spawned` runs a command in a folder, and fails with a `CommandFailed` when the command cannot start or exits non-zero. When the tool itself is stopped, `spawned` stops the command it was waiting for, so no build runs on after the release that started it. `capture` gives back what the command printed, and `run` lets the command print to the terminal.

A `CannotRelease` is the tool's other failure. It carries its reason as a sentence a person can act on.

=>

class CommandFailed extends Data.TaggedError('CommandFailed')<{
  readonly command: string
}> {}

class CannotRelease extends Data.TaggedError('CannotRelease')<{
  readonly reason: string
}> {}

const spawned = (
  command: ReadonlyArray<string>,
  folder: string,
  stdout: 'pipe' | 'inherit',
): Effect.Effect<string, CommandFailed> => {
  const failed = new CommandFailed({ command: command.join(' ') })
  return Effect.tryPromise({
    try: async (signal) => {
      const child = Bun.spawn([...command], { cwd: folder, stdout, stderr: 'inherit', signal })
      const text = stdout === 'pipe' ? await new Response(child.stdout).text() : ''
      return { code: await child.exited, text: text.trim() }
    },
    catch: () => failed,
  }).pipe(
    Effect.flatMap(({ code, text }) => (code === 0 ? Effect.succeed(text) : Effect.fail(failed))),
  )
}

const capture = (command: ReadonlyArray<string>, folder: string): Effect.Effect<string, CommandFailed> =>
  spawned(command, folder, 'pipe')

const run = (command: ReadonlyArray<string>, folder: string): Effect.Effect<void, CommandFailed> =>
  Effect.asVoid(spawned(command, folder, 'inherit'))

## The commit a release is built from

A dev release is built from a commit, never from the files of the working tree. The commit is the one named on the command line, by a hash, a branch, or a tag, and it is `HEAD` when the command line names none. `commitOf` asks Git for the full hash behind a name, and fails when the name is no commit.

Building from a commit keeps an unfinished edit out of every release. One case of that would surprise, though. A person who runs the tool over uncommitted changes and names no commit most likely means to release those changes, and a release of `HEAD` would leave them out. So `committed` refuses a release that names no commit while a tracked file holds an uncommitted change. A person who names a commit has chosen what to release, `HEAD` included, and the tool releases it whatever the working tree holds. A file Git does not track belongs to no commit, and it does not count.

=>

const named = Option.filter(Option.fromNullishOr(process.argv[2]), (name) => name !== '')

const commitOf = (name: string): Effect.Effect<string, CommandFailed> =>
  capture(['git', 'rev-parse', '--verify', `${name}^{commit}`], workspace)

const committed: Effect.Effect<void, CommandFailed | CannotRelease> = capture(
  ['git', 'status', '--porcelain', '--untracked-files=no'],
  workspace,
).pipe(
  Effect.flatMap((changes) =>
    Option.isNone(named) && changes !== ''
      ? Effect.fail(
          new CannotRelease({
            reason:
              'the working tree has uncommitted changes, and a release of HEAD would leave them out; commit them, or name the commit to release, as in `bun run release-local HEAD`',
          }),
        )
      : Effect.void,
  ),
)

## The number a dev release carries

A dev release is a prerelease of the version that follows the one the book carries. While the book carries 0.0.20, its dev releases are `0.0.21-dev.1`, `0.0.21-dev.2`, and so on. Semantic versioning sorts each of them before 0.0.21 itself, so the public 0.0.21 supersedes every dev release that led to it. `nextPatch` gives the version that follows the book's, and fails when the book's version is not three numbers.

The number after `dev` counts the dev releases of that version, and the registry keeps the count. `registryAnswer` sends the registry one request and gives back the status it answers with and the JSON beside it. A registry that does not answer at all is not running, and the tool fails with the command that starts it.

`heldVersions` asks the registry for every version it holds of one package. The registry answers with a document whose `versions` object has a key for each version. A package the registry has never seen has no such document, the registry answers 404, and the package holds no versions. Any other answer without that document is a fault in the registry, and the tool fails with the status. Read as no versions, such a fault would restart the count at one, over numbers the registry already holds.

The function `devNumber` reads the number out of one version, when that version is a dev release of the version asked about. The function `devVersion` gives the number one above the highest that any of the eight packages holds. Counting across all eight keeps a number from being used twice. A release that failed halfway has published its number for some packages, and the next release steps over that number for all of them.

=>

const nextPatch = (version: Option.Option<string>): Effect.Effect<string, CannotRelease> =>
  pipe(
    version,
    Option.flatMap((text) => Option.fromNullishOr(/^(\d+)\.(\d+)\.(\d+)$/.exec(text))),
    Option.map(([, major, minor, patch]) => `${major}.${minor}.${Number(patch) + 1}`),
    Option.match({
      onNone: () =>
        Effect.fail(
          new CannotRelease({ reason: 'the version variable is not a version of three numbers' }),
        ),
      onSome: Effect.succeed,
    }),
  )

const HeldPackageSchema = Schema.Struct({
  versions: Schema.Record(Schema.String, Schema.Unknown),
})

const heldPackage = Schema.decodeUnknownOption(HeldPackageSchema)

type RegistryAnswer = {
  readonly status: number
  readonly body: unknown
}

const registryAnswer = (
  path: string,
  request?: RequestInit,
): Effect.Effect<RegistryAnswer, CannotRelease> =>
  Effect.tryPromise({
    try: async (signal): Promise<RegistryAnswer> => {
      const answer = await fetch(`${registry}${path}`, { ...request, signal })
      const body: unknown = await answer.json().catch(() => undefined)
      return { status: answer.status, body }
    },
    catch: () =>
      new CannotRelease({
        reason: `no registry answers at ${registry}; start it with \`bun run registry\``,
      }),
  })

const heldVersions = (name: string): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<string>, CannotRelease> =>
  registryAnswer(`/@athrio%2f${name}`).pipe(
    Effect.flatMap(({ status, body }) =>
      status === 404
        ? Effect.succeed<ReadonlyArray<string>>([])
        : Option.match(heldPackage(body), {
            onNone: () =>
              Effect.fail(
                new CannotRelease({
                  reason: `the registry answered ${status} when asked which versions of @athrio/${name} it holds; its own output says why`,
                }),
              ),
            onSome: ({ versions }) => Effect.succeed<ReadonlyArray<string>>(Object.keys(versions)),
          }),
    ),
  )

const devNumber = (base: string, version: string): Option.Option<number> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(/^(.+)-dev\.(\d+)$/.exec(version)),
    Option.filter(([, of]) => of === base),
    Option.map(([, , number]) => Number(number)),
  )

const devVersion = (base: string, held: ReadonlyArray<string>): string => {
  const numbers = Array.getSomes(held.map((version) => devNumber(base, version)))
  return `${base}-dev.${Math.max(0, ...numbers) + 1}`
}

## A checkout beside the workspace

The tool builds the release in a checkout of its own, in a fresh folder under the system's temporary folder. `besideMade` makes that folder and names what goes into it: the checkout, the packed packages, and a settings file for npm. `checkedOut` adds the checkout as a Git worktree at the commit, detached from every branch, and installs its packages against the committed lockfile.

`besideRemoved` clears all of it away when the release is done, whether it succeeded or failed. It asks Git to remove the worktree and then removes the folder. A release stopped halfway can leave Git unable to remove the worktree, and the repository would then keep a record of a checkout whose folder is gone. So `besideRemoved` ends by asking Git to prune every such record. No step of the clearing stops the others, since each may find its work already done.

The checkout holds the commit's files, and those carry the last public version. The function `versioned` gives the checkout the dev release's version the way a public release gets its own. The command `loom set version` writes the version into the workspace's configuration and tangles the book again. The `loom` it runs is the one installed on this machine, the tangler of record, which each dev release brings up to date as its last step. The version then stands in every manifest, and in the code that reads it too, such as the `serviceVersion` at which the command line installs its language services. So the command line of a dev release asks for the services of that same dev release, and it knows from its version that it is a dev build.

Only the checkout changes. The working tree keeps the last public version in every manifest, and no commit records the number of a dev release.

`versioned` then installs once more. The packages name one another as `workspace:*`, and Bun writes a real version in place of that as it packs a package. Bun reads the version from the lockfile, which still records the last public one. The second install brings the lockfile's record of the workspace's own packages up to the dev release's version.

=>

type Beside = {
  readonly folder: string
  readonly checkout: string
  readonly packs: string
  readonly settings: string
}

const besideMade: Effect.Effect<Beside, CannotRelease, FileSystem.FileSystem> =
  FileSystem.FileSystem.pipe(
    Effect.flatMap((fs) => fs.makeTempDirectory({ prefix: 'loom-dev-release-' })),
    Effect.mapError(
      () =>
        new CannotRelease({ reason: "no folder could be made under the system's temporary folder" }),
    ),
    Effect.map((folder) => ({
      folder,
      checkout: join(folder, 'loom'),
      packs: join(folder, 'packs'),
      settings: join(folder, 'npmrc'),
    })),
  )

const checkedOut = (beside: Beside, commit: string): Effect.Effect<void, CommandFailed> =>
  run(['git', 'worktree', 'add', '--detach', beside.checkout, commit], workspace).pipe(
    Effect.andThen(run(['bun', 'install', '--frozen-lockfile'], beside.checkout)),
  )

const besideRemoved = (beside: Beside): Effect.Effect<void, never, FileSystem.FileSystem> =>
  Effect.ignore(run(['git', 'worktree', 'remove', '--force', beside.checkout], workspace)).pipe(
    Effect.andThen(FileSystem.FileSystem),
    Effect.flatMap((fs) =>
      Effect.ignore(fs.remove(beside.folder, { recursive: true, force: true })),
    ),
    Effect.andThen(Effect.ignore(run(['git', 'worktree', 'prune'], workspace))),
  )

const versioned = (beside: Beside, version: string): Effect.Effect<void, CommandFailed> =>
  run(['loom', 'set', 'version', version], beside.checkout).pipe(
    Effect.andThen(run(['bun', 'install'], beside.checkout)),
  )

## An executable compiled from the published bundle

The standalone executable is compiled from the bundle that the command-line package ships, as Part VIII explains. Packing that package runs its `prepack` script, which writes the bundle to `dist/main.js` in the checkout. Once the seven packages are packed, the bundle on disk is therefore the very bundle inside the packed command line. The function `compiled` compiles the executable from it with the package's `compile` script. That script compiles the grammar from the commit's own sources, compiles the bundle and the grammar into `build/loom`, and signs the file ad hoc so that `codesign` accepts it.

A public release runs the executable's smoke test on every platform it ships, and a dev release does the same on the machine that makes it. So `compiled` then runs the smoke test against the new file, before anything is published. The executable carries the dev release's version, so the smoke test also runs the cases that only a dev build can run: the ones where it upgrades from the registry with no flag, reports that it is behind, and tells a person that a newer dev release is out. A case that fails stops the release.

=>

const cliFolder = (beside: Beside): string => join(beside.checkout, 'packages', 'loom')

const compiled = (beside: Beside): Effect.Effect<void, CommandFailed> =>
  run(['bun', 'run', 'compile'], cliFolder(beside)).pipe(
    Effect.andThen(
      run(
        ['bun', 'test', './smoke/binary.smoke.ts', './smoke/install.smoke.ts'],
        cliFolder(beside),
      ),
    ),
  )

## The executable as a package

The registry holds packages, so the executable travels as one. The function `executablePacked` makes a folder for the package beside the checkout, copies `build/loom` into it, and writes the package's manifest beside it.

The manifest names the package and gives it the dev release's version. Its `os` and `cpu` fields name the system and the architecture that the executable runs on, so a package manager on any other machine refuses to install it. Its `files` field lists the executable alone. Bun then packs the folder into the folder of packed packages, where the file takes the name that `published` looks for, as each of the other seven does.

=>

const executablePacked = (
  beside: Beside,
  version: string,
): Effect.Effect<void, CannotRelease | CommandFailed, FileSystem.FileSystem> => {
  const folder = join(beside.folder, executablePackage)
  const manifest = {
    name: `@athrio/${executablePackage}`,
    version,
    description: "Loom's standalone executable for one platform, from a dev release.",
    license: 'MIT',
    os: [process.platform],
    cpu: [process.arch],
    files: ['loom'],
  }
  const written = Effect.gen(function* () {
    const fs = yield* FileSystem.FileSystem
    yield* fs.makeDirectory(folder)
    yield* fs.copyFile(join(cliFolder(beside), 'build', 'loom'), join(folder, 'loom'))
    yield* fs.writeFileString(
      join(folder, 'package.json'),
      `${JSON.stringify(manifest, null, 2)}\n`,
    )
  })
  return written.pipe(
    Effect.mapError(
      () =>
        new CannotRelease({ reason: `the executable's package could not be written to ${folder}` }),
    ),
    Effect.andThen(run(['bun', 'pm', 'pack', '--destination', beside.packs], folder)),
  )
}

## Publishing to the local registry alone

A package reaches the registry in two steps, and the tool takes the first step for all eight packages before it takes the second for any.

The first step packs a package. The function `packed` has Bun pack one of the seven source packages. The command `bun pm pack` runs the package's `prepack` script, which builds whatever the package ships built. It then writes the version in place of each `workspace:*`, and leaves the packed file in the folder of packed packages. The executable's package is packed once the executable is compiled and tested. A build or a test is the step most likely to fail. Packing everything first means a failed build stops the release with nothing published, where publishing as it went would leave some packages at the new number and the rest behind it.

The second step publishes the package. The function `published` has npm publish one packed file, named for the package and the version.

The publish must never reach npm's own registry. The person who runs the tool may well be signed in to npm, with the right to publish these very names. Three settings on npm's command line rule that out. `--registry` names the local registry. `--@athrio:registry` names it again for the scope, where npm would otherwise use a registry that a settings file gives the scope. `--userconfig` puts the file `settingsWritten` writes in place of the person's own settings file, so npm holds no token for any other registry while it publishes.

That file holds one line, the token npm shows the local registry. `signedIn` gets the token by signing in to the registry's one account, and the request that signs in also registers the account the first time. The request carries the name and the password twice. Verdaccio registers a new account from the request's body, and signs an existing account in from the `authorization` header.

The account's name and password stand in `account`, in plain sight. They guard nothing, since the registry answers only this machine, and whoever can run the tool there may publish with it. They exist because npm publishes only with a token, and Verdaccio honours only a token it issued.

Every dev release goes out under the `dev` tag. npm tags a publish `latest` unless it is told otherwise, and `latest` is the version a plain install resolves to. Under the `dev` tag, a plain install of a package npm holds still gets its last public release through the local registry, and a project gets a dev release only by naming its version.

The four source packages npm has never held are the exception, and so is the executable's package. The registry gives the first version of a package the `latest` tag, whatever tag the publish names, and later dev releases leave that tag where it is. So a plain install of one of those five gets its first dev release, and a project names the version for those as well. The command `loom upgrade` reads the executable's `dev` tag, never its `latest`.

=>

const account = { name: 'loom-dev', password: 'loom-dev' }

const SignedInSchema = Schema.Struct({ token: Schema.String })

const signedInAnswer = Schema.decodeUnknownOption(SignedInSchema)

const signedIn: Effect.Effect<string, CannotRelease> = registryAnswer(
  `/-/user/org.couchdb.user:${account.name}`,
  {
    method: 'PUT',
    headers: {
      'content-type': 'application/json',
      authorization: `Basic ${btoa(`${account.name}:${account.password}`)}`,
    },
    body: JSON.stringify(account),
  },
).pipe(
  Effect.flatMap(({ body }) =>
    Option.match(signedInAnswer(body), {
      onNone: () =>
        Effect.fail(
          new CannotRelease({
            reason: `the registry refused to sign in as ${account.name}; delete the htpasswd file in the registry's folder, start the registry again, and release again`,
          }),
        ),
      onSome: ({ token }) => Effect.succeed(token),
    }),
  ),
)

const settingsWritten = (
  beside: Beside,
  token: string,
): Effect.Effect<void, CannotRelease, FileSystem.FileSystem> =>
  FileSystem.FileSystem.pipe(
    Effect.flatMap((fs) =>
      fs.writeFileString(
        beside.settings,
        `${registry.replace('http:', '')}/:_authToken=${token}\n`,
      ),
    ),
    Effect.mapError(
      () => new CannotRelease({ reason: `npm's settings could not be written to ${beside.settings}` }),
    ),
  )

const packed = (beside: Beside, folder: string): Effect.Effect<void, CommandFailed> =>
  run(
    ['bun', 'pm', 'pack', '--destination', beside.packs],
    join(beside.checkout, 'packages', folder),
  )

const published = (
  beside: Beside,
  name: string,
  version: string,
): Effect.Effect<void, CommandFailed> =>
  run(
    [
      'npm',
      'publish',
      join(beside.packs, `athrio-${name}-${version}.tgz`),
      '--tag=dev',
      `--registry=${registry}`,
      `--@athrio:registry=${registry}`,
      `--userconfig=${beside.settings}`,
      '--ignore-scripts',
    ],
    beside.folder,
  )

## Bringing this machine's loom along

Dev releases are made on this machine and nowhere else, so a release is the one moment when the `loom` installed here falls behind. The release therefore ends by upgrading it. The effect `loomUpgraded` runs `loom upgrade --dev` with the `loom` a terminal runs, the same command a person would type. That `loom` asks the registry for the executable just published, checks the download against the checksum the registry recorded, and puts the new executable in place of itself. So one command installs every dev release, whether a release or a person runs it.

A dev build upgrades from the registry even without the flag. The flag lets the step serve a `loom` from a public release as well, which joins the dev releases when it upgrades. A `loom` that a package manager installed belongs to that package manager, so it names the package manager's command for a person to run, and changes nothing.

The step runs after the release has published all eight packages and cleared its checkout away. When the step fails, the release still stands, and the failure names the command to run again.

=>

const loomUpgraded: Effect.Effect<void, CommandFailed> = run(
  ['loom', 'upgrade', '--dev'],
  workspace,
)

## The release

The release runs from the commit to the registry in one pass. It resolves the commit and checks the working tree. It asks the registry what it holds and signs in before it builds anything, so a registry that is not running stops the release in a moment. Then it makes the checkout, reads the book's version there, and works out the dev release's number. It sets that version and packs the seven source packages. It compiles the executable, runs its smoke test, and packs it as the eighth package. Only then does it write npm's settings and publish the eight, in order. It prints the version a project pins, clears the checkout away, and last of all upgrades this machine's `loom` to the release.

A failure at any step stops the release and names itself: a command that failed, or the reason the release cannot be made.

=>

const release = Effect.gen(function* () {
  const config = yield* LoomConfig
  yield* committed
  const commit = yield* commitOf(Option.getOrElse(named, () => 'HEAD'))
  const held = yield* Effect.forEach(releasedPackages, heldVersions)
  const token = yield* signedIn
  yield* Effect.acquireUseRelease(
    besideMade,
    (beside) =>
      Effect.gen(function* () {
        yield* checkedOut(beside, commit)
        const { variables } = yield* config.resolve(beside.checkout)
        const base = yield* nextPatch(Option.fromNullishOr(variables.version))
        const version = devVersion(base, held.flat())
        yield* Console.log(`releasing ${version} from ${commit}`)
        yield* versioned(beside, version)
        yield* Effect.forEach(devPackages, (folder) => packed(beside, folder))
        yield* compiled(beside)
        yield* executablePacked(beside, version)
        yield* settingsWritten(beside, token)
        yield* Effect.forEach(releasedPackages, (name) => published(beside, name, version))
        yield* Console.log(`published ${version} to ${registry}`)
      }),
    besideRemoved,
  )
  yield* loomUpgraded
}).pipe(Effect.provide(LoomConfig.layer), Effect.provide(BunServices.layer))

const failWith = (message: string): Effect.Effect<void> =>
  Console.error(`release-local: ${message}`).pipe(
    Effect.andThen(
      Effect.sync(() => {
        process.exitCode = 1
      }),
    ),
  )

BunRuntime.runMain(
  release.pipe(
    Effect.catchTags({
      CommandFailed: (error) => failWith(`command failed — ${error.command}`),
      CannotRelease: (error) => failWith(error.reason),
    }),
  ),
)

## Pinning a dev release

A project pins a dev release by its exact version, and tells its package manager where the registry is in one of two ways. It names the registry for one install, as `bun add @athrio/loom@0.0.21-dev.1 --registry http://localhost:4873` does. Or it gives the `@athrio` scope that registry in its own `.npmrc` or `bunfig.toml`, and then only the packages of the scope come from the local registry. Either way the registry must be running whenever an install has to fetch.

The command line of a dev release needs the registry once more, when `loom add` or `loom update` installs a language service or the tropes. It asks for the package at its own version, which only the dev registry holds, and it runs the package manager in the store's folder, where the project's `.npmrc` does not apply. So a dev build names the dev registry to the package manager itself, as Part VII describes, and the registry must be running for that install as well.

